    {
      "id": "Xref",
      "code_hash": "337316d4dbf43cd8dbf85fc77e318811",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><h2 id=\"2-the-rule-and-its-contract\">2. The rule and its contract</h2>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">We'll write the rule in Ibis, state what it assumes and what it\npromises, and have the checker prove it before any data runs. The rule\nis plain Ibis, and the decorator writes down what everyone involved\ncounts on:</span>\n<ul>\n<li><code>requires</code> is what the job assumes about its input. Transaction ids\n  are unique, amounts are not negative, and timestamps are before 2100,\n  so that adding five minutes can't overflow.</li>\n<li><code>ensures</code> is what the refund queue relies on. There is one row per\n  pair, the first transaction comes before its duplicate, every row has\n  a merchant and a non-negative amount, and the duplicate is no earlier\n  than the first.</li>\n</ul>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">The checks in <code>ensures</code> are the ones you would write as dbt tests\n(<code>unique</code>, <code>not_null</code>, <code>accepted_range</code>, an expression test) and run on\nyesterday's data. Here the checker proves them for every input that\nmeets <code>requires</code>, before the job runs.</span></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
    {
      "id": "SFPL",
      "code_hash": "aca0d037dbc70cde3c44b62eab8c0ee0",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
